// Shared instructions for a single admitted worker, not a Relay retry policy.
export const PROGRESS_BOUNDED_EXECUTION = `Progress-bounded execution:
Carry the complete admitted Task objective through investigation, implementation, corrections and relevant tests in this execution Step. Ordinary technical obstacles, failed tests and internal phases do not require another Step or owner handoff. After resolving a blocker or finding, continue toward the original goal and its remaining requirements; fixing that obstacle alone does not complete the Task. Iteratively inspect evidence, diagnose failures, make bounded corrections and rerun relevant validation while each next action is justified and authorized. There is no fixed correction-count default. Repeating the same failure or action without new evidence is not progress.
Stop and preserve useful work at an evidenced capability, authority, policy, trust or safety boundary, when a material choice needs its owner, or when no justified diagnostic or corrective action remains. An uncertain technical hypothesis calls for useful in-scope investigation before a terminal handoff. Unknown prior execution state, ambiguous external mutation, uncontained prior execution, and repository, credential, security or production authority uncertainty remain stop conditions for work depending on that state or boundary.
For fresh schema-v4 independent source/research attempts, prior-state holds apply to the specific shared checkout, physical execution resource, exclusive environment or external effect that cannot be proven independent. A missing unrelated Outcome alone does not block an independent attempt or prove an older attempt canceled. Preserve all original attempt identities and one-call-per-attempt replay protection.
Before every terminal return, including blocked, reconcile task-owned worktree state when safe: preserve useful changes in ordinary task commits, leave unrelated work untouched, and remove only confirmed disposable generated residue or add justified repeatable-artifact ignores without hiding useful work. Inspect actual ownership and permissions when distinguishing task work from generated residue; the worker and collector may have different access. Do not reset, blindly commit, publish, permission-repair or retry arbitrary residue. Report remaining uncommitted/non-ignored work, its known cause and the exact reconciliation or access boundary in the summary. Material execution warnings remain decision inputs for the next owner/review boundary even after a successful durable handoff. Automatic workers hand local commits to trusted Writer; this instruction grants no publication authority.
A tool's explicit private mode, including atomic replacements and generated boot indexes, need not be widened for collection. After containment, the fixed trusted Writer reads task-owned source through its admission-bound collector, including 0600 files and 0700 directories. Preserve source permissions; never widen access to unknown or private content or use symlinks/hard links to import outside-workspace data. Older installations without this capability may still report a precise collector access blocker. Previously installed native workspace-write launchers protect the root .agents directory: EROFS there is not proof that the whole checkout is read-only. Preserve the admitted launch identity and any native/admin/host restriction; a writable nested disposable projection is fixture qualification, not delivery into the actual project Skill directory. New owner-adopted danger-full-access launchers remove the inner native sandbox only, without granting Unix, publication or TEST/PROD authority.
Disposable TEST state wholly inside an execution's explicitly isolated sandbox follows Relay's TEST Environment Authority (jresearchsoftware/codex-relay, docs/execution-policy.md#test-environment-authority), which scopes the generic stop, preservation and cleanup rules above.
If using the single shared TEST contour, first claim its host-local marker through scripts/test-environment-lock.py as documented in docs/test-environment-lock.md. A claim is sufficient allocation within existing TEST authority; a competing holder means BLOCKED with its Task identified and no TEST access. Keep the claim for the entire TEST use, restore usable TEST and release only your exact execution's claim on completion or handled failure. Interrupted claims require confirmed stopped ownership before recovery; age alone grants no takeover. Non-TEST work needs no claim, and this rule grants no new host access or credentials.
Subagent use is discretionary and evidence-driven within the resolved Subagents permission. Select each subagent's model and reasoning effort independently based on its assignment's complexity and risk; they may differ from the parent's. By default provide a separate self-contained assignment containing its goal, necessary facts/files, constraints and completion criteria, without automatically passing full conversation or execution history. Include broader history only when materially necessary, preserving relevant prior decisions, constraints, prohibitions and protected boundaries. Delegation never expands Task/CR scope, external authority, credential access, publication or production rights. It does not authorize nested Relay workers or execution fan-out. This policy does not authorize blind Relay retries, new Relay model executions, publication, merge or deployment. Keep the admitted parent model and effort fixed and report validation truthfully.`;

